# Welcome to the baremetal.nvim tutor

This tutor walks you through everything this config adds on top of plain
Neovim. It takes about 15 minutes. Lines marked ✗ are exercises: edit them
until the mark turns into ✓.

New to Vim itself? Do the built-in tutor first: `:Tutor`{vim}

Table of contents:

- [Lesson 1: The leader key](*leader*)
- [Lesson 2: Finding files and text](*finding*)
- [Lesson 3: Moving around fast](*moving*)
- [Lesson 4: Editing superpowers](*editing*)
- [Lesson 5: Code intelligence (LSP)](*lsp*)
- [Lesson 6: Build, run and debug](*run*)
- [Lesson 7: Git](*git*)
- [Lesson 8: Windows, buffers and terminal](*windows*)
- [Lesson 9: Toggles and sessions](*toggles*)
- [Lesson 10: Making it yours](*custom*)

Press `<Enter>`{normal} on any link above to jump to it.

# Lesson 1: THE LEADER KEY *leader*

** The leader key is `<Space>`. Press it and wait. **

Almost every command in this config starts with `<Space>`. You do not have to
memorise anything: press `<Space>` and wait a moment, and a popup
(which-key) lists every key you can press next, grouped by topic:

    <Space>f   find          <Space>c   code (LSP)
    <Space>s   search        <Space>d   debug
    <Space>g   git           <Space>r   run
    <Space>b   buffers       <Space>u   UI toggles
    <Space>x   diagnostics   <Space>m   CMake

  1. Press `<Space>` now and look at the popup. Press `<Esc>` to close it.

  2. Press `<Space>c` and wait. These are the code commands you will use
     every day.

  3. Press `<Space>sk` to search through *all* keymaps by name. Type `harpoon`
     to see the harpoon keys, then `<Esc>`.

# Lesson 2: FINDING FILES AND TEXT *finding*

** One fuzzy finder for everything: `<Space><Space>`. **

    <Space><Space>   smart find (files, recent, buffers in one list)
    <Space>ff        files in the project
    <Space>fr        recently opened files
    <Space>fb        open buffers        (also <Space>,)
    <Space>/         grep the whole project
    <Space>sw        grep the word under the cursor
    <Space>sR        search & replace across the project
    <Space>sr        resume the last search

Inside any picker: type to filter, `<C-j>`/`<C-k>` to move, `<Enter>` to
open, `<C-v>` to open in a vertical split, `<Esc>` to close.

  1. Press `<Space>fc` to list the config files. Type `keymaps` and press
     `<Enter>`. Come back with `<C-o>`{normal}.

** Two file explorers. **

    <Space>e    tree on the left (neo-tree), `l` opens, `h` closes a folder
    -           oil: the current folder as an editable buffer

  2. Press `-`{normal}. You are now editing the folder as text: rename a file by
     changing its line, delete it with `dd`, create it by typing a new line.
     Changes are applied when you save with `:w`. Press `-` again to go up,
     `<Esc>` or `:q` to leave without changes.

** Harpoon: pin the 4-5 files you are working on. **

    <Space>H        pin the current file
    <Space>h        show the pinned list (reorder / delete like text)
    <Space>1 … 5    jump straight to pinned file 1 … 5

# Lesson 3: MOVING AROUND FAST *moving*

** Press `s` and two characters to jump anywhere on screen. **

Press `s`{normal}, type the first two characters of where you want to go,
then press the label letter that appears there.

  1. Use `s` to jump onto the `X` below, then press `x` to delete it.

The quick brown fXox jumps over the lazy dog. [[The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.]]

  2. `S`{normal} selects a whole syntax node with labels. It shines in real
     code: try `S` in a C or Rust file and pick a label to select a whole
     `if`, function call or block.

** Jump by code structure (in code files with a treesitter parser). **

    ]f  [f    next / previous function       ]F [F   end of function
    ]c  [c    next / previous class/struct
    ]a  [a    next / previous argument
    ]]  [[    next / previous use of the word under the cursor
    ]d  [d    next / previous diagnostic     ]e [e   errors only
    ]h  [h    next / previous git change
    ]t  [t    next / previous TODO comment
    [x        jump up to the enclosing function/module (sticky header)

`<C-d>`, `<C-u>`, `n` and `N` keep the cursor centred on the screen.

# Lesson 4: EDITING SUPERPOWERS *editing*

** Surround: add, delete and replace brackets and quotes. **

    gsa{motion}{char}   add       gsaiw"   wraps a word in "quotes"
    gsd{char}           delete    gsd(     removes the ( ) around the cursor
    gsr{old}{new}       replace   gsr'"    turns 'x' into "x"

  1. Put the cursor on `hello` and type `gsaiw"` to wrap it in quotes.

say hello to everyone [[say "hello" to everyone]]

  2. Put the cursor inside the inner parentheses and type `gsd(`.

print((value)) [[print(value)]]

  3. Put the cursor inside the quotes and type `gsr'"`.

let name = 'baremetal' [[let name = "baremetal"]]

  4. In visual mode, `gsa` wraps the selection. Select `x + y` with `v` and
     type `gsa)`.

total = x + y * 2 [[total = (x + y) * 2]]

** Move lines with `<A-j>` / `<A-k>`. **

  5. Put the cursor on the line that says `second` and press `<A-k>` to
     move it up so the list is in order. In visual mode `<A-j>`/`<A-k>` move
     the whole selection.

    second
    first
    third

** Text objects for code (need a treesitter parser, so try them in a C,
   Rust, Python, ... file). **

    af / if    a function / inside a function     `vaf` selects it
    ac / ic    a class or struct
    aa / ia    an argument                         `cia` changes it
    ai / ii    a conditional (if)
    al / il    a loop
    ak / ik    a comment
    <Space>cx  swap the argument with the next one (<Space>cX: previous)

  6. Open any code file, put the cursor inside a function and try `vaf`,
     then `daa` on an argument, then `u` to undo.

** Other small helpers. **

    <Space>p (visual)   paste over a selection without losing what you yanked
    < and > (visual)    indent and keep the selection
    <C-s>               save from any mode
    <Space>U            undo tree   (<Space>su: search undo history)

Brackets and quotes close automatically while you type in insert mode.

# Lesson 5: CODE INTELLIGENCE (LSP) *lsp*

** Language servers, completion and formatting work out of the box. **

Open a source file in a language you installed. The status line shows which
language servers are attached; `<Space>cL` shows details.

    gd          go to definition          K           documentation popup
    gr          references                gK          signature help
    gD          declaration               <Space>ca   code action / quick fix
    gI          implementation            <Space>cr   rename symbol everywhere
    gy          type definition           <Space>cf   format (also on save)
    <Space>ci   who calls this function   <Space>cI   what this function calls
    <Space>co   outline of the file       <Space>ss   jump to a symbol
    <Space>cd   show the diagnostic on this line
    <Space>xx   all problems in the project (Trouble)

C / C++ only:

    <Space>ch   switch between header and source
    <Space>cA   AST view   <Space>cH   type hierarchy

Rust only:

    <Space>ce   explain the error    <Space>cM   expand macro
    <Space>rR   cargo runnables      <Space>rt   cargo tests

** Completion **

The menu opens while you type. `<Tab>`/`<S-Tab>` or `<C-j>`/`<C-k>` move,
`<Enter>` accepts, `<C-Space>` opens the menu by hand. Snippets jump between
fields with `<Tab>`.

NOTE: clangd needs a `compile_commands.json` to understand a C/C++ project.
      With CMake press `<Space>mg`. With Make run `bear -- make` once.

# Lesson 6: BUILD, RUN AND DEBUG *run*

** `<Space>rr` compiles and runs the current file. **

    <Space>rr   build + run in a terminal at the bottom
    <Space>rb   build only, errors go to the quickfix list (]q / [q)
    <Space>rd   build with debug info and start the debugger

It knows C (with AddressSanitizer + UBSan), C++, GAS assembly, Rust (or
`cargo run` in a Cargo project), Go, Python, Lua, Bash, and Verilog /
SystemVerilog testbenches (Icarus Verilog).

  1. Make a file `hello.c`: `:e /tmp/hello.c`{vim}, write a `main` that prints
     something, and press `<Space>rr`. Press `q` to close the output.

** Projects **

    <Space>oo   run a task (make, cargo, npm, just, ...)   <Space>ot  task list
    <Space>mg   CMake configure   <Space>mb  build   <Space>mr  run
    <Space>md   CMake debug       <Space>mt  choose target

** Debugger **

    F5          start / continue          F9          toggle breakpoint
    F10         step over                 F11         step into
    F12         step out                  <Space>dC   run to cursor
    <Space>du   toggle debug UI           <Space>de   evaluate expression
    <Space>dB   conditional breakpoint    <Space>dt   stop

A `.vscode/launch.json` in your project is picked up automatically. There is
also a "gdb: remote target" configuration for embedded boards (OpenOCD,
QEMU `-s -S`).

# Lesson 7: GIT *git*

    <Space>gg    lazygit (full git UI in a floating window)
    ]h  [h       next / previous changed hunk
    <Space>ghp   preview the hunk     <Space>ghs  stage it   <Space>ghr  reset it
    <Space>ghb   blame this line      <Space>ub   toggle inline blame
    <Space>gd    diff view of all changes      <Space>gH  history of this file
    <Space>gs    git status picker    <Space>gB   open the line on GitHub

# Lesson 8: WINDOWS, BUFFERS AND TERMINAL *windows*

    <C-h/j/k/l>       move between windows
    <Space>|  <Space>-   split right / split below
    <Space>wd         close window      <Space>wo  close all others
    <C-arrows>        resize
    <S-h>  <S-l>      previous / next buffer (the tabs at the top)
    <Space>bd         close buffer      <Space>bo  close all other buffers
    <Space>bb         switch to the last buffer
    <C-/>             toggle a terminal; `<Esc><Esc>` leaves terminal mode

  1. Press `<Space>|` to split, `<C-l>` / `<C-h>` to move, `<Space>wd` to close.

# Lesson 9: TOGGLES AND SESSIONS *toggles*

Everything under `<Space>u` switches something on or off:

    <Space>uf   format on save          <Space>uh  inlay hints
    <Space>ud   diagnostics             <Space>uw  line wrap
    <Space>us   spell checking          <Space>uC  pick a colour scheme
    <Space>z    zen mode                <Space>n   notification history

Sessions are saved automatically per folder. `<Space>qs` restores the last
session of the current folder, `<Space>qS` lets you pick one, `<Space>qq`
quits everything.

# Lesson 10: MAKING IT YOURS *custom*

Your personal changes live in `lua/custom/` inside the config folder
(`:lua print(vim.fn.stdpath("config"))`{vim}). That folder is ignored by git, so
`git pull` never conflicts with it.

    lua/custom/languages.lua   which languages get servers, parsers, plugins
    lua/custom/init.lua        your options, keymaps and autocommands
    lua/custom/plugins/*.lua   extra plugins, or overrides of existing ones

Example `lua/custom/init.lua`:
~~~ lua
vim.opt.relativenumber = false
vim.keymap.set("n", "<leader>W", "<cmd>wa<cr>", { desc = "Save all" })
~~~

Example `lua/custom/plugins/theme.lua`:
~~~ lua
return {
  { "catppuccin/nvim", name = "catppuccin", opts = { flavour = "macchiato" } },
}
~~~

To change the languages later, edit `lua/custom/languages.lua`, restart
Neovim and run `:Lazy sync`:
~~~ lua
return { "c", "rust", "python" }
~~~

Available: `c` `asm` `rust` `systemverilog` `vhdl` `python` `go` `bash` `web`.

That's it! Open this tutor again any time with `:Tutor baremetal`{vim} or the
`T` key on the start screen.
